{0 Alternatives}

An overview of property-based testing libraries available for OCaml
and how Hedgehog compares.

{1 QCheck}

{{:https://github.com/c-cube/qcheck}QCheck} is the most established
property-based testing library for OCaml, with a large ecosystem and
integrations with Alcotest and OUnit.

{b Approach:} Traditional QuickCheck model — generators and shrink
functions are separate. You write [QCheck.Gen.t] for generation and
[QCheck.Shrink.t] for shrinking.

{b Strengths:}
- Mature, battle-tested, widely adopted
- Rich combinator library
- Alcotest and OUnit integration out of the box
- Works with all OCaml versions (no OCaml 5 dependency)

{b Tradeoffs:}
- Manual shrink functions required for good shrinking on custom types
- Shrinking can produce values outside the generator's domain
- Composed generators don't automatically compose shrinking

{1 Jane Street Quickcheck}

{{:https://github.com/janestreet/ppx_quickcheck}ppx_quickcheck} is
part of the Jane Street ecosystem and integrates with Core.

{b Approach:} Uses a "Quickcheckable" type class pattern. Generators
and observers are derived via ppx for types annotated with
[[@@deriving quickcheck]].

{b Strengths:}
- Automatic generator derivation via ppx
- Deeply integrated with Core and Expect tests
- Supports both generation and observation (for function generation)

{b Tradeoffs:}
- Tightly coupled to the Jane Street ecosystem (Core, ppx_jane)
- Separate generation and shrinking model
- Less accessible if you don't already use Jane Street libraries

{2 Key differences from Hedgehog}

{3 Separate generators and shrinkers}

Jane Street Quickcheck keeps [Generator.t] and [Shrinker.t] as
separate types. Shrinkers have type ['a -> 'a list] — they eagerly
enumerate all candidate shrinks as a list. Hedgehog's rose tree
approach ([Tree.t] with lazy [Seq.t] children) means shrinking is
integrated into generation: composed generators ([let*]) automatically
compose their shrinking. In Quickcheck, you must ensure shrinkers are
properly derived for composite types, typically via ppx.

{3 PPX derivation}

Quickcheck's strongest feature is [[%quickcheck.generator: int list]]
— automatic generator derivation via ppx for any type annotated with
[[\@\@deriving quickcheck]]. Hedgehog has no ppx support; all
generators are written explicitly. This is more boilerplate but fully
transparent — you always know exactly what your generator does.

{3 Eager vs lazy shrinking}

Quickcheck's [Shrinker.t = 'a -> 'a list] eagerly enumerates all
shrink candidates. Hedgehog's [Tree.t] children are [Seq.t] — lazy
sequences that can represent infinite shrink strategies (e.g.
[Shrink.towards] for integers) without materialising them. This makes
Hedgehog more memory-efficient for deep shrink trees.

{3 Observers for function generation}

Quickcheck provides [Observer.t] for generating random {e functions}
— a feature Hedgehog does not have. Observers allow you to test
higher-order properties like [List.map f (List.map g xs) =
List.map (fun x -> f (g x)) xs] with randomly generated [f] and [g].

{3 Core ecosystem dependency}

Quickcheck requires Base, Core, Core_kernel, and ppx infrastructure.
Hedgehog depends only on OCaml >= 5.0 stdlib — zero external
dependencies. This makes Hedgehog easier to adopt in projects that
don't already use the Jane Street ecosystem.

{3 Expect_test integration vs algebraic effects}

Quickcheck integrates deeply with [Expect_test] — test output is
compared against expected output inline in source files. Hedgehog uses
OCaml 5 algebraic effects for assertions, annotations, and coverage
tracking. The effects-based approach allows collecting rich
counterexample context ([annotate], [cover], [classify]) alongside
failures without exception handling.

{3 No state machine testing}

Quickcheck is purely a generator/shrinker library with no built-in
state machine testing. Hedgehog's {!Hedgehog.Stm} module provides
both sequential and parallel linearizability checking out of the box.

{3 Subterm combinators}

Hedgehog provides [Gen.subterm], [subterm2], and [subterm3] for
recursive data structures. When shrinking [f x y], these try [x] and
[y] directly as candidates before trying [f x' y] or [f x y'].
This dramatically speeds up shrinking for ASTs and recursive
structures. Quickcheck has no equivalent — you must manually arrange
your shrinker to try subterms first.

{3 Golden ratio size scaling}

Hedgehog's [Gen.recursive] reduces the size parameter by the golden
ratio (0.618) at each recursion level to naturally control depth.
Quickcheck threads a size parameter but does not have this specific
mathematical scaling strategy.

{1 Hedgehog}

{b Approach:} Integrated shrinking via rose trees. Generators produce
{!Hedgehog.Tree.t} values where the root is the generated value and
children are shrunk alternatives.

{b Strengths:}
- Every generator shrinks automatically — no manual shrink functions
- Shrinking respects generator invariants (filters, preconditions)
- Range-controlled generation with explicit origins and scaling
- Algebraic effects for clean assertion/logging API (OCaml 5)
- Built-in state machine testing ({!Hedgehog.Stm})
- Parallel property checking via domainslib

{b Tradeoffs:}
- Requires OCaml 5.1+ (algebraic effects)
- Smaller ecosystem than QCheck
- No ppx derivation for generators (yet)
- Rose tree overhead for simple cases where shrinking isn't needed

{2 Key differences in depth}

{3 Integrated shrinking via rose trees}

The core type is [Gen.t = int -> Seed.t -> 'a Tree.t option]. Every
generator produces a rose tree where the root is the generated value
and children are shrunk alternatives. In QCheck, generators and shrink
functions are separate — you write [QCheck.Gen.t] for generation and
[QCheck.Shrink.t] for shrinking. This separation means shrinking can
produce values that violate generator invariants (e.g. a filtered
generator might shrink to a value that doesn't pass the filter).

{3 Recursive tree binding}

[Gen.bind] recursively binds through the {e entire} shrink tree, not
just the root value. This matches the semantics of Haskell Hedgehog's
[TreeT] monad: when you compose generators with [let*], shrinking
the first generator automatically re-runs the second generator on
each shrunk alternative. QCheck's separate model has no equivalent —
composed generators lose shrinking information.

{3 Tree.interleave for list shrinking}

List shrinking uses a sophisticated interleaving algorithm that
combines multiple strategies: removing elements (halving, then
individual removal), shrinking individual elements in place, and
shrinking pairs of elements simultaneously. The entire process uses
lazy [Seq.t] sequences to avoid materialising the (potentially
infinite) shrink tree. QCheck's list shrinking is comparatively
simple.

{3 Range-controlled generation}

[Range.t] separates bounds from origin, giving control over both
value distribution and shrink direction. [Range.linear 0 100]
generates values scaled by the size parameter and always shrinks
toward the origin (0). QCheck uses flat uniform distributions with
no built-in notion of size scaling or shrink targets.

{3 Splittable PRNG}

{!Hedgehog.Seed} wraps OCaml 5's [Random.State] — a splittable LXM
generator — behind an immutable interface, copying the state before
each operation. Splitting derives independent seeds for sub-generators
without threading mutable state, which is essential for deterministic
parallel shrinking. QCheck threads a single mutable [Random.State.t]
through generation rather than splitting it, so sub-generators cannot
be evaluated independently.

{3 Algebraic effects for assertions}

[Property.assert_], [annotate], [cover], [classify] and [collect] all
use [Effect.perform] under the hood. This keeps test logic cleanly
separated from generators — you write
[return (fun () -> assert_ ...; annotate ...)] rather than threading
assertion results through the generator monad. QCheck has no
equivalent; test outcomes are returned as values.

{3 Coverage checking}

Properties can require minimum coverage of labelled categories:
[cover 50.0 "positive" (n > 0)] fails the property if
fewer than 50% of test cases satisfy the condition. Coverage is
implemented via effect-based log accumulation in the property runner.
QCheck provides [collect] for reporting distributions but cannot
enforce minimum thresholds.

{3 State machine testing}

The {!Hedgehog.Stm} module supports both sequential and parallel
(linearizability) testing. Parallel testing generates command
sequences, runs them concurrently with [Domain.spawn], then checks
whether the observed results match {e some} sequential interleaving
of the model. QCheck has no built-in state machine testing.

{3 LCS-based diff for counterexamples}

When [===] or [diff] assertions fail, a line-level LCS diff algorithm
produces readable output showing exactly what changed between expected
and actual values. QCheck prints the counterexample value but does not
compute diffs.

{3 Pure shrink strategies}

[Shrink.towards], [Shrink.halves], and [Shrink.removes] are
composable building blocks that produce lazy sequences of shrunk
alternatives. These integrate directly with the rose tree structure so
that all generators shrink for free. In QCheck, equivalent strategies
must be manually composed into [Shrink.t] values and attached to each
generator.

{1 Feature comparison}

{table
  {tr {th Feature} {th QCheck} {th JS Quickcheck} {th Hedgehog}}
  {tr {td Integrated shrinking} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td Lazy shrink trees} {td No} {td No (eager lists)} {td Yes (Seq.t)}}
  {tr {td Range-controlled gen} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td PPX derivation} {td No} {td Yes} {td No}}
  {tr {td Function generation} {td No} {td Yes (Observer.t)} {td No}}
  {tr {td Subterm combinators} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td State machine testing} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td Parallel testing} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td Coverage enforcement} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td Effects-based API} {td No} {td No} {td Yes}}
  {tr {td Diff on failure} {td No} {td Via Expect_test} {td Yes (LCS)}}
  {tr {td OCaml < 5 support} {td Yes} {td Yes} {td No}}
  {tr {td External dependencies} {td None} {td Core/Base/ppx} {td None}}
  {tr {td Alcotest integration} {td Built-in} {td No} {td Separate package}}
}

{1 Choosing a library}

- {b Use QCheck} if you need broad compatibility, don't use OCaml 5 yet,
  or want the largest ecosystem and community.
- {b Use Jane Street Quickcheck} if you are already in the Jane Street
  ecosystem and want ppx-derived generators.
- {b Use Hedgehog} if you want the best shrinking, need state machine
  testing, or prefer the effects-based API.
